      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "Best Practices for Twilite Graph Building",
        "description": "The practices that keep a Twilite graph coherent, composable, and safe to evolve, each traced to Twilite's own contracts.",
        "scope": "Authoring graph artifacts: declarations and identity, presentation, relationships and ports, cross-graph composition, deriving from templates, and evolving and validating graphs. It does not cover the runtime scripting model or renderer internals.",
        "overview": "Twilite's contracts are small and explicit, so good graphs mostly come from a few habits: begin from a declaration, let relationships carry meaning, connect only through declared ports, make authority explicit, derive from templates by preserving structure, and validate the whole path from structure through save and reload. This topic collects those habits, explains why each works, and cites the contract behind it.",
        "keyQuestions": "Where does a graph's identity live? How much presentation should a graph author? What makes a relationship meaningful rather than decorative? How does one graph safely use another's capabilities? How should a graph be derived from a template, and changed later, without breaking it?",
        "documentation": "This topic summarizes and interprets Twilite's contracts; it does not replace them. Where it and a current contract disagree, the contract is authoritative. Companion topics: Twilite Graph Model explains why the model stays coherent, and Twilite Graph Primitives explains the primitives these practices arrange.",
        "definitionKey": "topic",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "topic",
          "sourceRef": "github://mikemartinez1974/public/templates/topic-template/classes/nodes/topic.node-class.node",
