          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 15
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "Declaration-first identity",
        "definition": "A graph declares who it is, what kind of artifact it is, and which surfaces it exposes before anything else is authored around it.",
        "documentation": "The Declaration carries `identity.graphId`, `name`, `version`, and `description`, plus `intent.kind`. The declaration node id anchors the graph id; nodes created after it inherit that graph id, while imported or pasted nodes keep their own. A blank graph may be explored without a declaration, but canonical authorship begins once one exists. A graph's title and kind live in the declaration, not in standalone title or type nodes.",
        "examples": "A new graph starts with a declaration named for its subject and an `intent.kind` describing what it is, such as `documentation` or `plan`; its views and content come after.",
        "notes": "`field` nodes hold functional data inside the artifact. They are never used for the graph's own name, kind, or identity.",
        "definitionKey": "topic-concept",
        "_classBinding": {
