        },
        {
          "id": "claims",
          "label": "Claims",
          "direction": "output",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 15
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "Relationships and ports",
        "definition": "Edges connect nodes through declared ports, and the relationship, not a label or a position, carries the meaning.",
        "documentation": "An edge needs a unique id and existing source and target nodes. Ports, when given, must be declared on the nodes; omitted ports mean `root`. A label is human-readable, not semantic logic. When a relationship's meaning should be reusable, an edge class (`data.edgeClassRef` with a matching `edgeClassKey`) is the canonical semantic upgrade; plain edges remain valid without one.",
        "examples": "In an idea graph, `idea.supports` runs from evidence to the assumption it changes confidence in. A generic reference edge would lose that direction and meaning.",
        "notes": "Handles are visual affordances for ports. Placing a handle does not create a new semantic endpoint.",
        "definitionKey": "topic-concept",
        "_classBinding": {
