      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "Deriving from templates",
        "definition": "A template is an executable starting point: derive a graph by copying its structure, retargeting identity, and replacing its example content.",
        "documentation": "A template package is its root graph plus its adjacent node classes, used together as one versioned package. Deriving from it preserves the declaration, the landing surface, the Views, the Glyph, and the bridge authority for every custom node type, while the graph's own subject replaces the starter content.",
        "examples": "This topic was derived from the Topic template: it keeps the class bridges and the Topic, Concept, Claim, Mechanism, Perspective, Source, and Synthesis roles, and replaces the example content.",
        "notes": "Starter relationships are demonstrations, not facts about the new graph. Each one is kept, retargeted, or removed.",
        "definitionKey": "topic-concept",
