          "angle": 90
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "A graph should declare itself before it accumulates structure",
        "claim": "Author the Declaration first: a graph's identity, kind, and exposed surfaces belong there, and everything else attaches to it.",
        "rationale": "Identity in one place gives every node a stable owner, because nodes created after the declaration inherit its graph id, and it lets workspaces and other graphs address the artifact without inspecting its contents. A standalone title or type node describes the graph from inside its own body, where it can drift from the declaration.",
        "evidence": "The native graph baseline states that a Twilite graph is fundamentally a declaration, that graph title belongs in `declaration.identity.name` and graph kind in `declaration.intent.kind`, and that older title and type nodes describing the graph itself are migration targets.",
        "caveats": "A blank graph may be explored without a declaration. The rule applies once the graph is becoming a saved artifact.",
        "documentation": "See the Declaration-first identity concept.",
        "confidence": "high",
        "definitionKey": "topic-claim",
