        },
        {
          "id": "source",
          "label": "Source",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 90
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "A type name grants nothing; authority is a declared Bridge",
        "claim": "Use a Bridge, not a type name, to give a graph authority over another graph's capability. Listing a node type in `dependencies.nodeTypes` only describes the dependency.",
        "rationale": "Authority that is only implied cannot be reviewed or revoked. A Bridge is a visible node in the graph, so a reader can see which capabilities the graph imports or exposes.",
        "evidence": "The idea template states that `dependencies.nodeTypes` does not grant runtime class authority. The model inventory says Export Bridges expose structural capability, Import Bridges consume it and must be backed by the corresponding incoming edge, and unconnected import-looking creation affordances are Ports, not Bridges.",
        "caveats": "Grants, scope, and exposure policy are defined by the bridge contracts, which this topic does not summarize.",
        "documentation": "See the Cross-graph composition concept.",
        "confidence": "high",
        "definitionKey": "topic-claim",
