        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "title": "Derive from a template by preserving its structure and retargeting identity",
        "claim": "Copy a template's structure, retarget every identity it owns, keep its bridges and class bindings, and replace the starter content, keeping only the relationships that are true.",
        "rationale": "The template's structure is what makes a derived graph resolve, navigate, and render. Retargeting identity keeps the new graph from being owned by the template, and replacing content keeps it from asserting things nobody authored.",
        "evidence": "The idea template's instructions list the derivation steps: copy the root structurally, retarget graph, declaration, View, landing, Glyph, GitHub, content, and bridge identities, preserve bridges and bindings, replace starter content, and re-evaluate every starter relationship. The topic template's instructions say its root and adjacent node classes are one versioned package and that a derived graph preserves the declaration, its surfaces, and bridge authority for every custom node type.",
        "caveats": "A template's embedded class bindings can lag its node classes, so check a derived graph against the current class definitions.",
        "documentation": "See the Deriving from templates concept and the How derivation preserves structure mechanism.",
        "confidence": "high",
        "definitionKey": "topic-claim",
